      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Nine cases of emergency proximal row carpectomy are analysed. In 3 cases, the resection was performed in the context of transcarpal amputation in which the bone shortening obtained by resection allowed revascularisation without a flap. The functional result was poor because of the severity of the initial lesion. In the other 6 cases, consisting of proximal row bone defects or so-called intreatable fracture-dislocations, the results in terms of pain, range of movement and strength were similar to those obtained with elective operations."},
